, restrict-qualified pointers, manual 4x unrolling preserving exact summation order, and a single OpenMP parallel region spanning all iterations with SIMD worksharing loops will reduce thread-creation overhead, improve cache locality, enable vectorization across bodies, and keep the force/position update barrier minimal—outperforming any individual transformation.\",\n  \"notes\": \"The fused velocity+force loop is separated from position updates by an implicit barrier to avoid data races on positions. Static scheduling balances uniform per-i work. The output is reconstructed into the original Body layout for byte-exact comparison.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [\n    \"10af5e5f3ddc484d\",\n    \"82ca35f9753444de\",\n    \"9adc054f311d4298\"\n  ],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Converted body storage from array-of-structs to structure-of-arrays (SoA)\",\n    \"Added restrict qualifiers to all position/velocity pointer parameters\",\n    \"Manually unrolled the inner j-loop by a factor of 4 while preserving exact summation order\",\n    \"Inlined force computation into main and used a single OpenMP parallel region spanning all iterations\",\n    \"Used #pragma omp for simd schedule(static) on both force+velocity and position-update loops\"\n  ]\n}",
