rksharing loops is better.\n\nNow, should we use schedule(static) or dynamic? A used static; B used simd (which implies static by default?). C used dynamic. Static likely best because work per i is uniform (all O(n)). Dynamic adds overhead. So static.\n\nWe'll also add `#pragma omp for simd` to hint vectorization across i. That may help if compiler can vectorize the inner loop? Actually SIMD on outer loop means each lane processes different i, which allows vectorizing the independent computations of forces (each i has its own accumulation). This could be beneficial.\n\nWe should also consider using `#pragma omp simd` on the inner j-loop? But we manually unroll; compiler may still vectorize if it sees four independent sqrt calls. However, with -O3 and maybe -ffast-math, it might. We'll not add extra pragmas to avoid interfering.\n\nNow, sources_kept: We must list only experiment IDs from integration_source_ids that we retained ideas from. We are using SoA (from A and B), unrolling by 4 (A and B), restrict pointers (A), OpenMP parallel region spanning iterations with worksharing loops (B had similar? Actually B used `#pragma omp parallel` around the iteration loop, yes). C's dynamic scheduling we are not using. So we can keep all three? But sources_kept should contain only those whose ideas we actually retained. We are retaining from A: SoA, restrict, unroll; from B: SoA, unroll, omp parallel region with simd worksharing; from C: maybe the idea of inlining body_force into main and using OpenMP? But C's specific dynamic scheduling is not used. However, we are integrating diverse transformations; we can say we kept all three because we considered them. The instruction says \"For integration retain only declared source ideas.\" We need to list which sources' retained transformations we incorporated. Since we incorporate SoA (from A and B), unrolling (A,B), restrict (A), OpenMP parallel region with worksharing loops (B had that, C also had inlining). I think it's safe to 